--TEST--
`Core\Uri::encodeFormValue` is deliberately not idempotent — it escapes the `%` it writes, so over the ASCII sweep encoding twice is a fixed point for exactly the 65 bytes it passes through and differs for the other 63, one decode peels exactly one layer for all 128, and the space that became a `+` is re-encoded as `%2B` rather than left alone — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 2.1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An encoder that skipped a byte already looking escaped — the "smart" encoder
// that leaves `%41` alone — would pass every neighbouring case: it is still
// inverse to its decoder over single bytes, still disagrees with
// `encodeComponent` on exactly two, still passes 65 through. What it breaks is
// text that legitimately contains a `%`, which is the commonest way a form
// value gets corrupted in transit. So the property worth pinning is that
// applying the encoder twice is *visible* and reversible, not that it is a
// no-op.
//
// PHP 8.5's `urlencode` behaves the same way, and the two-step round trip below
// was checked against `urldecode(urldecode(urlencode(urlencode($c))))` for the
// whole sweep.

int $fixedPoints = 0;
int $changedAgain = 0;
int $peelsOneLayer = 0;
int $twoStepRoundTrip = 0;
int $b = 0;
for (; $b <= 127; $b = $b + 1) {
    string $c = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($b as uint);
    string $once = Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($c);
    string $twice = Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($once);

    // A byte the set passes through is unchanged however many times it goes
    // round; every other byte's output holds a `%` or a `+`, both of which the
    // encoder then has something to say about.
    if ($twice == $once) {
        $fixedPoints = $fixedPoints + 1;
    } else {
        $changedAgain = $changedAgain + 1;
    }

    // One decode undoes one encode and no more — the layers do not collapse,
    // which is what makes a double encode recoverable rather than merely
    // detectable.
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($twice) as string) == $once) {
        $peelsOneLayer = $peelsOneLayer + 1;
    }
    if ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($twice) as string)) as string) == $c) {
        $twoStepRoundTrip = $twoStepRoundTrip + 1;
    }
}
echo "bytes 128, encoding twice is a fixed point for ", $fixedPoints,
     " and changes ", $changedAgain, "\n";
echo "one decode peels one layer ", $peelsOneLayer, "/128, two decodes recover the byte ",
     $twoStepRoundTrip, "/128\n";

// The witnesses, so the counts have rows behind them. The space is the
// interesting one: its first encoding is a `+`, which is not a `%` at all, and
// the encoder still has an answer for it — `%2B`, the escape a literal `+`
// takes — so the two spellings of a space do not fold into one another under a
// second pass.
array<string> $witnesses = [" ", "%", "+", "~", "a", "%20"];
foreach ($witnesses as string $witness) {
    string $first = Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($witness);
    echo "[", $witness, "] -> [", $first, "] -> [", Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($first), "]\n";
}

// And the bug the property exists to keep recoverable, read from the wire's
// side: a value encoded twice and decoded once is *not* the original, and says
// so — a program that reads it once gets the escape text back rather than a
// plausible near-miss, and one more decode is the whole repair.
string $typed = "50% off + tax";
string $doubled = Core\Uri::encodeFormValue(Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($typed));
echo "[", $doubled, "]\n";
echo "[", (Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($doubled) as string), "]\n";
echo "[", (Core\Uri::decodeFormValue((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($doubled) as string)) as string), "]\n";
echo "recovered ", ((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue((Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($doubled) as string)) as string) == $typed) as string, "|\n";
--EXPECT--
bytes 128, encoding twice is a fixed point for 65 and changes 63
one decode peels one layer 128/128, two decodes recover the byte 128/128
[ ] -> [+] -> [%2B]
[%] -> [%25] -> [%2525]
[+] -> [%2B] -> [%252B]
[~] -> [%7E] -> [%257E]
[a] -> [a] -> [a]
[%20] -> [%2520] -> [%252520]
[50%2525%2Boff%2B%252B%2Btax]
[50%25+off+%2B+tax]
[50% off + tax]
recovered 1|
